pg_btree_compact
================

The pg_btree_compact module provides functions to reduce B-tree index bloat
by merging sparsely populated leaf pages.

Overview
--------

B-tree indexes can become bloated when many tuples are deleted or updated.
While PostgreSQL's VACUUM can mark pages as deleted when they become completely
empty, partially empty pages remain in the index structure, wasting space.

pg_btree_compact addresses this by merging adjacent leaf pages that together
don't exceed a configurable threshold, allowing the freed pages to be recycled.

Functions
---------

btree_compact(index regclass, min_items int DEFAULT 10, max_items int DEFAULT NULL)
  Compacts the specified B-tree index by merging sparse pages.
  
  Parameters:
    - index_name: The index to compact
    - min_items_per_page: Pages with fewer items are candidates for merge
    - max_items_per_page: Target pages can't exceed this (default: based on fillfactor)
  
  Returns statistics about the operation including pages merged and time spent.

btree_compact_estimate(index regclass, min_items int DEFAULT 10)
  Estimates potential space savings without modifying the index.
  
  Returns statistics about sparse pages and estimated savings.

Locking Strategy
----------------

The compaction process uses a two-phase locking approach:

1. Planning Phase (ShareLock):
   - Scans the index to identify merge candidates
   - Builds a merge plan
   - Duration: Seconds to minutes depending on index size

2. Execution Phase (AccessExclusiveLock):
   - Executes the pre-built merge plan
   - Performs actual page modifications
   - Duration: Milliseconds to seconds

This minimizes the time spent holding an exclusive lock.

Example Usage
-------------

-- Check current index size
SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('my_index'));

-- Estimate potential savings
SELECT * FROM btree_compact_estimate('my_index');

-- Perform compaction
SELECT * FROM btree_compact('my_index');

-- Check new size
SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('my_index'));

Limitations
-----------

- Only works on B-tree indexes
- Requires brief exclusive lock on the index
- Only merges leaf pages (not internal pages)
- Processes page pairs independently

When to Use
-----------

Use pg_btree_compact when:
- You have bloated indexes (check with btree_compact_estimate)
- You want partial space reduction without full REINDEX
- You can tolerate a brief exclusive lock
- You want a quicker alternative to REINDEX CONCURRENTLY

Comparison with Alternatives:
- REINDEX: Full rebuild, longer lock time, optimal space reduction
- REINDEX CONCURRENTLY: No blocking, but very long duration and resource intensive
- pg_repack: External tool, creates index copy, requires extra disk space
- pg_btree_compact: Brief lock, partial reduction, no extra space needed

